WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:22.800
He

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.950
is the only person whose baby picture was
flown into space, and whose body has been

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.650
preserved for decades after his death.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:34.090
He pushed his version of Communism so far,
and for so long, that he was able to overthrow

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.480
the government of the largest country in the
world.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.129
To some, he is a hero, and to others, he is
one of the most evil villains in history.

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:46.899
On Today’s Biographics, we are talking about
Vladimir Lenin- The man behind the Bolshevik

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:52.050
Revolution, and the founder of the Soviet
Union.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:59.620
Early Life
The year was 1870, and Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.210
was born in a Russian provincial town called
Simbirsk.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.670
He would eventually grow up to be most known
by his alias, “Vladimir Lenin” later in

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.390
life, and we will explain the origin of the
name later.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:13.659
Most revolutionaries have a tragic backstory
of a young life of hardship, rising from the

00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:17.189
lower classes to demand better treatment for
the downtrodden.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:20.549
And many people are taught that this is the
case with Lenin, as well.

00:01:20.549 --> 00:01:25.290
They try to say that his parents were descended
from serfs, and that he had to study hard

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.441
to break into the middle class.

00:01:27.441 --> 00:01:32.320
Buxt the truth was that Lenin’s parents
were actually financially comfortable, and

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.869
by all accounts, he had a happy childhood.

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:38.719
His mother, Maria, was descended from a wealthy
Jewish family who was Swiss-German, so she

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:40.890
taught him French and German at home.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.090
His father, Ilya, was the Director of Public
Schools for the entire province.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:50.280
They were considered to be “hereditary nobility”,
and they were far more educated than the average

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.280
Russian.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.649
This placed his parents in a group called
the “Intelligentsia”, who would debate

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:57.780
over philosophy and politics in their spare
time.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.659
Many of the members of the Intelligentsia
became politicians who helped shape the new

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:06.439
laws of the nation, while others devoted their
life to pushing for a Russian revolution.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:09.790
Vladimir and his older brother Alexander were
no exception.

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:13.750
His brother loved studying science in High
School, but there were no opportunities to

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.930
become a scientist in his small Russian village
like Simbirsk.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.380
So Alexander moved to St. Petersburg to attend
university.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.550
During this time, he joined a political study
group, and they began to discuss the failures

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:29.610
of the Romanov empire, and they believed that
the only way to save Russia would be to remove

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.760
them from power.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:36.120
When he was 21 years old, Alexander joined
a terrorist group who attempted to assassinate

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.870
Tsar Nicholas II with a homemade bomb.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:43.850
The terrorists were caught, and Alexander
and his friends were hanged for their crime.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:48.959
Vladimir Lenin was 17 at the time that his
brother died, and just about to graduate High

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:49.959
School.

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:53.680
Even though he had gone through such a shocking
tragedy, he still managed to become the valedictorian

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.050
of his graduating class.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.400
He went to law school with the intention of
becoming a lawyer.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.580
But just a few months into his first semester,
he joined a demonstration of students who

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.210
were protesting against the school rules.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:08.310
Since the Russian police were already watching
Lenin closely to see if he was also a terrorist

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:14.040
like his brother, the school saw his participation
in the demonstrations as a sign that he might

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.560
cause a lot of trouble.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:25.990
So, he was expelled, and decided to educate
himself, instead.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.120
The Sparks That Ignited a Revolution
The Tsar, Nicholas II, released the peasant

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.610
class from their serf status, meaning that
they no longer had to be indentured servants

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.210
to wealthy landowners.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:38.129
While this helped relieve the difficulties
of a lot of people, it wasn’t a perfect

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:41.810
solution to the problems that the peasant
class was facing.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:46.079
People were still in debt, and many struggled
to make ends meet.

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:51.189
The vast majority of people were illiterate,
and Russia was decades behind in progress

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:52.970
compared to the western world.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:57.400
Members of the Intelligentsia were very unhappy
with the way things were going, and wanted

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:02.080
their country to move forward with progress,
just like the rest of the human race.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.400
Lenin studied at law school, but he realized
that with his bad nerves, and his family’s

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.599
bad reputation from his brother being a terrorist
would make it impossible for him to get a

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:11.670
job as a lawyer.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:16.260
Vladimir Lenin began his self-education by
reading Karl Marx and other revolutionary

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:17.260
writers.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.890
He joined a Marxist study group, where intellectuals
met and discussed philosophy, and the possibility

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:23.360
of a Russian Revolution.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.520
It was around this time that a major famine
spread through the land near his home.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:33.130
Records of the 1891 famine vary drastically,
with some stating there were 300,000 deaths,

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.080
while others say it was closer to 5 million
people dying of starvation.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.300
Lenin was fine, of course, since his family
was well-off, and he had plenty of money to

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.509
buy food.

00:04:41.509 --> 00:04:44.740
He did nothing to offer aid to the people
dying around him.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:50.009
He was actually happy about this, saying that
the worse things got in Russia, the better

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:51.240
it will become.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.419
He knew that people needed to start dying
in the thousands before people angry enough

00:04:56.419 --> 00:04:57.880
to want a revolution.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:03.440
Karl Marx wrote that he believed that an industrialized
society would be most likely to create a revolution,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.180
because he believed they will want to rise
against commercialism.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.780
Vladimir Lenin rewrote a lot of what Marx
was actually saying.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.510
Lenin wrote the idea that the revolutionary
Intelligentsia will guide the uneducated peasants.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.600
Even though the philosophy was labeled as
“Marxism” all throughout the reign of

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:25.200
the Soviet Union, it is now called “Leninism”,
because it really was his own personal ideas,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.770
not those of Karl Marx.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.340
Lenin joined a socialist group that gained
access to a factory, where they started handing

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.009
out fliers to factory workers.

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:37.660
They were trying to make people angry about
their work situations, and want to demand

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.070
shorter hours and higher pay.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:44.040
They were caught, though, because they accidently
let a police informant join the group, and

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.040
they were all arrested.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.700
At the time, any form of revolutionary activity
was taken very seriously, and they all had

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:50.860
to serve time in jail.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.400
He was 26 years old at the time he went to
prison, but it was nothing like what we imagine

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:56.770
a Russian prison to be.

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:00.669
Since he descended from a noble bloodline,
he was given a very comfortable place to live,

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:06.030
where he could wear whatever clothes he wanted,
read books, and received daily visits from

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.030
family and friends.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:11.180
He was even allowed to continue writing his
revolutionary pamphlets and essays while he

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:13.700
was behind bars.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:18.690
After 14 months in jail, he was exiled to
Siberia for three years.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.270
In a way, this was actually great for him,
because he was given a government pension

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.259
for his living expenses.

00:06:24.259 --> 00:06:28.520
He spent all of his time reading and writing
about the revolution, which is what he wanted

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.520
to do, anyway.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.940
So, for him, this time in Siberia was more
like a very long writer’s retreat, and he

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:36.750
was actually very productive during his time
away.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:40.539
His girlfriend was also arrested, and sent
to Siberia, too.

00:06:40.539 --> 00:06:43.530
They got married, so that they could live
there in exile together.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:48.400
While Vladimir Lenin was writing his revolutionary
ideas in Siberia, he would send them back

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.120
to his socialist friends, who would publish
his essays in their secret newspaper.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.870
He made up a variety of pen names, so that
it seemed like multiple intellectuals were

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:00.090
all pushing for the same revolution, but it
was really just him.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.889
The pen name he eventually stuck with was
“Vladimir Lenin”, which is what he is

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:05.259
remembered as today.

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:08.740
After being released from his time of exile,
he was 30 years old.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:12.741
He and his wife moved to Munich, Germany,
where they could print their revolutionary

00:07:12.741 --> 00:07:16.400
newspapers in Russian without getting in trouble
with the German police.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.270
He would then have the papers smuggled into
Russia, with the help of fellow revolutionaries

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:21.979
who carried them in their luggage.

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:27.229
One of these co-conspirators was a young man
named Joseph Stalin, who was equally passionate

00:07:27.229 --> 00:08:13.300
about the coming revolution, and would eventually
take Lenin’s place.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:09:10.710
The Bolshevik Revolution
In most tellings of the Russian Revolution,

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.790
Vladimir Lenin’s rise to power always seems
very straight-forward.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:18.650
He is always depicted as a well-respected
intellectual who was able to rise to power

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:19.650
almost immediately.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.460
But that’s very far from the truth.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:26.630
His revolution was a very long process, but
he never gave up trying to slither his way

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:27.750
into a place of power.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:31.140
Vladimir Lenin had a real talent for stirring
up trouble.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:34.900
Even amongst the Intelligentsia, he would
call anyone who disagreed with him his “enemies”,

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:38.030
and the sniveling “bourgeoise”.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:42.470
Even though they were all middle-class socialists,
he had absolutely no patience for anyone who

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:44.860
did not agree with him 100%.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.810
During one of their meetings, to discuss their
strategies about the revolution, he insulted

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:53.060
some of the socialists so strongly, they got
up and left.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:56.860
While they were gone, he declared that his
ideals were the “Bolsheviks”, or the “Majority”,

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:00.240
while everyone else was the “Menshevik”
or “minority” party.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.790
The name “Bolshevik” stuck from then on.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:07.980
In 1904, Lenin got a bit stressed out from
all of this debating and discussing his philosophy

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:14.190
and handing out pamphlets which made him ill,
so took any entire year-long hiking holiday

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:18.600
in the Swiss alps with his wife, but the Bolsheviks
continued to do their own revolutionary work

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:19.640
while he was gone.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:24.290
In 1905, there was a peaceful protest in St.
Petersburg in front of the winter palace.

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.980
People held up signs pleading for better working
conditions.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:29.190
All they wanted was an 8-hour work day.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:32.680
They even sang “God Save the Tsar” to
show their love and support for the royal

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:33.680
family.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.570
But instead of listening to their request,
Tsar Nicholas II ordered his guards to shoot

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:38.950
at the crowd.

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:42.440
Thousands of people died, and it became known
as “Bloody Sunday”.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:47.410
People were outraged, and began protesting
for workers rights all across Russia.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:51.350
When Lenin heard the news, he abruptly ended
his vacation in Switzerland and decided to

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.580
come back to Russia.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:54.990
He finally had the opportunity he had been
waiting for.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:59.540
He began to preach to people that they needed
to organize and take action against the Tsar.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:03.150
He got a little too excited about the idea
of a bloody revolution, and wrote how they

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:08.460
should pour hot acid on the royal guard, and
got into specific details about how to murder

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:12.100
someone... which was something that no other
revolutionary writer had done before.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:16.850
However, there were still not enough revolutionaries
willing to fight and kill people on Lenin’s

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:17.850
behalf.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:21.990
Many of the Bolshevik revolutionaries were
exiled to Siberia, and Lenin fled to Switzerland

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:24.220
before he could be arrested, too.

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:28.980
World War I put a pause on everyone’s revolutionary
plans.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:32.940
Tsar Nicholas II left St. Petersburg behind
to join his men on the front lines.

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:38.310
Nicholas left his wife, Alexandra, in the
care of their spiritual advisor, Grigori Rasputin.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:43.020
This man believed that he was the second coming
of Christ, and he believed that he had magic

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:45.190
healing powers given to him by God.

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:49.990
Many of the decisions Rasputin made were harshly
criticized, like putting his unqualified friends

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:57.750
in powerful positions.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:01.940
Lenin still continued to write about his revolutionary
ideas from Switzerland, and encouraged soldiers

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.220
to turn their guns against their officers
during the war.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:09.770
But almost no one was actually reading the
underground newspapers, except for the handful

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:11.080
of active Bolsheviks.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:15.240
The vast majority of the Russian people were
eager to defend their country, but the war

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.500
was truly a disaster.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:21.270
Millions of people died, inflation ruined
Russia’s economy, and women were standing

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:22.380
in long bread lines.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:27.240
Tsar Nicholas II renamed St. Petersburg to
“Petrograd”, as a symbol of his devotion

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:28.240
to the Russian people.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:32.180
For years, people had criticized the name
“St. Petersburg” because it sounded too

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:35.320
German, and “Petrograd” was much more
Russian.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:40.300
Tsar Nicholas believed that this would somehow
prove his loyalty, but that gesture wasn’t

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:41.300
enough.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:45.230
For the first time, thousands of Russian people
were trained in the military and knew how

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:46.790
to use guns.

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:50.470
Soldiers and police officers were just as
angry as the rest, and people were swarming

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:55.890
the palace to take over the Tsar all on their
own….And Lenin had nothing to do with it.

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:57.000
He wasn’t even there.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:01.650
He was still in Switzerland, and he was panicking,
because the revolution was starting without

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:02.650
him.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:05.700
The political parties were split into two
groups- the provisional government, which

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.610
was made up of the representatives called
the Duma, and the Worker’s Soviets, who

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:14.280
were controlled by the trade unions and made
up of the peasants, soldiers, and factory

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:15.280
workers.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:18.960
Lenin knew that he was no match for the provisional
government’s leader, Alexander Kerensky.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:22.010
He was a seasoned lawyer, and a master of
public speaking.

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:26.420
Kerensky resonated with the Duma, and since
Lenin was not actually a lawyer, he knew he

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:28.550
would lose in a political debate against him.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:34.760
So he focused his efforts on trying to manipulate
the lesser educated Worker’s Soviets, instead.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:38.370
When he arrived at the train station, Lenin’s
friends were there to greet him, and he hardly

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:42.570
said “hello” before standing up on a pedestal
to shout at the crowd that they had done an

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:46.620
awful job with the revolution, and that it
wasn’t going to be over until they took

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.600
down the provisional government.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:53.270
Almost no one actually agreed with him, and
went about their business.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:57.180
Even his fellow Bolsheviks no longer wanted
to print his essays, because it was such an

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.180
unpopular opinion.

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:02.310
The provisional government was leading Russia
back to war against Germany, and World War

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:03.730
I was still going on.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.570
But soldiers were not happy to serve.

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:08.640
It was the reason why they had overthrown
the Tsar in the first place.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:09.910
People wanted the war to be over.

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:14.510
Many of them abandoned their posts on the
battlefield, or completely refused to go in

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:15.510
the first place.

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:19.790
Lenin tried to stand up again and lead the
crowds of peasants, but someone from the provisional

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:22.800
government shouted that he was a German spy.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.630
This made the crowd turn against him, and
he had to run away.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:29.170
Alexander Kerensky could see that he was going
to cause a lot of trouble for the authority

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:33.790
of the provisional government, so he outlawed
the existence of the Bolshevik party.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.600
Lenin needed to escape.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:39.210
He put on a bad wig, shaved his beard, and
got on the next train to Finland.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:43.390
Vladimir Lenin spent a very long time hiding
on a Finnish farm, continuing to write his

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:45.810
ideas and send them back to Russia.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:50.380
Since no one was actually listening to him
or agreeing with his ideas, he decided to

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:52.270
take it by force.

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:56.180
One night in October of 1917, he snuck back
into Russia, and met up with his commerads

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:57.180
in Petrograd.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:00.870
By this time, he had convinced more members
of the Worker’s Party to join his side.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:04.670
He convinced his Bolshevik followers of the
Red Guard to break into the Winter Palace

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:05.790
for a coup-d’etat.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.810
They captured the members of the provisional
government at gunpoint.

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:14.140
The Duma were arrested, taken to jail, and
Vladimir Lenin became the new leader of Russia

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:17.140
without ever being voted in.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:23.930
Lenin’s Violent Reign
Vladimir Lenin addressed the crowd of Russian

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:28.120
peasants the morning after storming the winter
palace, and introduced himself as their new

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:31.160
dictator, and the leader of The Soviet Union.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:36.650
Lenin promised them exactly what they wanted,
in the simplest words possible; “peace,

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:37.950
bread, and land.”

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:41.070
One of his first acts as leader was to end
the war with Germany.

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:44.520
People were happy, because that’s exactly
what they wanted all along.

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.940
Right from the beginning, he isolated himself
at the Kremlin in Moscow, so that he could

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.060
continue writing.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:54.270
The Bolshevik party wasted no time spreading
propaganda that Vladimir Lenin was like a

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:59.220
kind old grandfather who only wanted the best
for the Russian people, and that they didn’t

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:06.060
have to worry anymore, because he was there
to take care of them.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:11.270
In reality, he was bloodthirsty, and wanted
his revolution to be as violent as possible.

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:16.240
He told tenants to kill their landlords, and
encouraged violence against the upper classes.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.251
He gave out the order to his Red Guard to
remove the Romanov royal family from their

00:16:20.251 --> 00:16:21.251
palace.

00:16:21.251 --> 00:16:26.250
At that time, the number of men who joined
in Lenin’s Red Guard had grown to 200,000,

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:29.350
and he had a small army to stand behind him.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:33.670
Many of the Russian people protested, because
they loved the Romanovs, especially the young

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:34.670
children.

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:37.620
At first, Lenin lied, saying that they would
be kept somewhere safe.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:41.380
But once he realized that they were too much
of a threat to his power, he ordered their

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:42.380
execution.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:45.860
Since the family was being held in Siberia,
it took a very long time for the public to

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:46.940
learn about their deaths.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:50.410
At first, people believed it was just Tsar
Nicholas who was killed, and that the women

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:55.400
and children were spared, it would take years
for the public to learn the truth of his brutality.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:56.400
Lenin began implementing his communist government.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:00.570
He proclaimed that every Russian citizen should
have good rations, but in order to do that,

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.390
the farms needed to be controlled by the government.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:07.390
Farmers were not allowed to keep or sell the
food they grew, and all of the crops were

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:09.750
spread out among the entire nation.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:13.280
People could no longer choose their jobs,
and were given employment in newly constructed

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:15.370
factories based on the needs of the country.

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:19.620
Some people were very happy about this, and
it gave them a sense of security.

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:24.170
Statues and paintings of him were erected
around Petrograd, and many called him a hero.

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:26.380
Not everyone was drinking Lenin’s kool-aid,
of course.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:30.850
Plenty of people were furious with him for
ordering the death of innocent Romanov children.

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:34.610
In 1918, a woman named Fanya Kaplan attempted
to assassinate Vladimir Lenin.

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:36.620
She shot him twice at close range.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:39.840
He was seriously injured, but he still managed
to survive.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:44.130
Kaplan was arrested, and she refused to give
up the name of any of her co-conspirators.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.900
She was executed in September of that same
year.

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:50.430
Lenin would recover from his injuries in the
hospital, but from that day forward, he showed

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:55.940
no mercy to anyone who expressed even the
smallest opinion opposing his rule.

00:17:55.940 --> 00:18:00.060
Even though he always encouraged the peasant
class to stand up for their rights and rebel

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:04.940
against their government, it was no longer
okay to do so once it was his government.

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:09.780
Any time peasants or farmers tried to protest
against his new rules, they were killed without

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:10.780
mercy.

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:14.690
Those who opposed Lenin didn’t go down without
a fight, though.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:18.520
In 1918, a group called The White Army battled
against the Red Army in a civil war, fighting

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.130
to take down the Soviet Union.

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:24.450
However, the White Army was not organized
very well, and they lost the war.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:28.740
In 1920, farmers were angry that their grain
was being stolen from them for the “greater

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:30.430
good” of the Communist nation.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:34.380
This began what is known as the Tambov Rebellion,
but they were all gassed by the Red Army,

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:37.080
and their crops were taken away from their
dead hands.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:41.540
At least 50,000 people were interned, mostly
women, children, and the elderly - some of

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:43.800
them sent to the camps as hostages.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:47.790
There were several other incidents like this,
and any rebellion among the Russian people

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.270
was quickly squashed.

00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:54.080
Even after trying to make his political philosophy
work through threats of death and destruction,

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:55.860
Lenin knew when to call it quits.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:59.740
When millions of people were on the brink
of dying from drought and famine, he decided

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:04.200
to temporarily stop communism, and allowed
farmers to keep the food they planted, and

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:09.140
sell the excess food for profit….Y’know,
a little thing called Capitalism.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:10.950
This was a huge success.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:14.000
People were no longer starving, and the economy
improved.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.710
Once things had stabilized again, Lenin went
back to forcing food rations.

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:21.970
Unfortunately, not every leader who followed
in Lenin’s footsteps knew to take a break

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:24.020
from communism during times of famine.

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:27.930
And Lenin’s successor, Joseph Stalin, would
be remembered as one of the worst dictators

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:29.610
who ever lived.

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:38.220
Death and Legacy
When he was 54, Vladimir Lenin’s health

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.500
began to decline, and he had multiple strokes.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:44.600
But even when he was in poor health, he was
trying to write in a journal to give commands

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:45.600
from his bed.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:49.620
When he died in 1924, people were shocked,
and they were not expecting him to die so

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:50.620
young.

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:53.300
Hundreds of thousands of people showed up
to his funeral, even though it was well below

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:54.680
freezing temperatures.

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:58.930
People were sobbing, upset that the one man
who truly cared about them was now gone.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:02.480
He did manage to make some improvement in
the economy, and there was so much pro-Lenin

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:06.990
propaganda that had been circulated, that
people believed that he was truly their savior.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:11.160
Five days after Lenin passed away, the city
of Petrograd was renamed “Leningrad” in

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:12.160
his honor.

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:16.650
His body was embalmed so that people could
continue to visit him and pay their respects.

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:20.800
Vladimir Lenin’s reputation only became
more powerful after his death than when he

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:21.800
was alive.

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:25.590
He was like a legend who managed to change
not just Russia, but several other countries

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:27.110
throughout the world.

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:30.780
Communist leaders looked to him for inspiration
to run their own dictatorships.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:35.760
The USSR continued for decades after his death,
and despite all of its setbacks, the system

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:40.020
lead to more progress than Russia had seen
in hundreds of years.

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:44.630
Before Lenin, the country was very far behind
the rest of the western world in terms of

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:46.810
industrialization, education, and modernization.

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:51.330
If it were not for all of the reforms made
by the Soviet Union, they may have never gotten

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:56.380
so far with the Space Race against the United
States, and they still may be lagging behind

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:57.610
today.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:02.000
In 1991, the Soviet Union collapsed, and all
of the statues of Lenin were torn down.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:05.580
Instead of seeing him as a hero, he is now
remembered as a villain.

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:08.040
Leningrad became St. Petersburg once again.

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:12.270
There is no telling how far the nation would
have progressed if it were not for the creation

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:16.910
Soviet Union, but one thing is for sure- Without
Vladimir Lenin, the world would be a very

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:33.250
different place today.

